💡 **SUGGESTION: the terminal "connection lost" message drops the underlying error**

In `_drain`'s catch-all (bin/self_manage.dart:390), the exhausted-resume outcome discards the error object:

```dart
} catch (_) {
  received = builder.length;
  final giveUp = _resumeOrGiveUp(
    'connection lost at ${_formatBytes(received)} '
    '($maxResumeAttempts resume attempts exhausted)',
  );
```

while the headers-phase path in `_open` does keep it (`_resumeOrGiveUp('$error')`, bin/self_manage.dart:331). After a flaky link exhausts the budget mid-body, the user and any bug report see `fa: download failed (network): connection lost at 800 KB (3 resume attempts exhausted)` with no hint whether this was a `Connection reset by peer`, a `ClientException`, or something else — the exact detail needed to debug the corporate-link class of failures this card targets.

Two related points, one fix:

1. Interpolate the error into the exhausted message: `'connection lost at … ($n resume attempts exhausted): $error'`. (Keep the stable `'connection lost at 800 KB'` prefix if tests match on it — the test at test/cli/self_manage_test.dart:2076 uses `contains`, so appending is safe.)
2. The bare `catch (_)` also routes programmer errors (`TypeError`/`ArgumentError` thrown from `builder.add`/`onProgress`) into the resume policy, silently retrying them 3× and masking them as a network death. The "never throws" contract (review r1) is worth keeping — but consider logging the unexpected type when it is not an `Exception` (e.g. a `_warn`-style line or a `log` note) so a future bug surfaces instead of looking like a bad link day.

Polish — does not block merge.

💡 **SUGGESTION: a stream error delivered after all promised bytes wastes the archive and fails with a pointless 416**

`run`'s completeness check (bin/self_manage.dart:424–432) only runs on a CLEAN drain return:

```dart
final total = totalBytes;
if (total != null && received < total) { … resume … }
```

If the transport errors the stream at/after the promised byte count (a reset racing the final byte, a misbehaving proxy that over-sends then dies), `_drain`'s catch treats it as a mid-body death, burns a resume attempt, and sends `Range: bytes=<received>-`. A well-behaved server answers **416 Range Not Satisfiable**, which `_open` maps to `ArchiveDownloadOutcome.http(416)` — the update fails with `download failed (HTTP 416)` although the complete archive is already sitting in `builder`.

This is unreachable on well-formed content-length HTTP (dart:io completes the stream at exactly `contentLength` bytes, so an error cannot surface after the boundary), which is why it is a suggestion, not a blocker — but the guard is three lines and removes the whole class:

```dart
} catch (error) {
  received = builder.length;
  final total = totalBytes;
  if (total != null && received >= total) {
    elapsed = _wall.elapsed; // every promised byte arrived; ignore the tail error
    return (null, false);
  }
  …
}
```

Fail-closed today (provenance would never see truncated bytes), so no correctness risk either way — purely wasted work and a misleading error message on a rare race. Optional polish.

💡 **SUGGESTION: `_drainBody` leaks armed timers if `stream.listen` throws synchronously**

bin/self_manage.dart:218–222:

```dart
armStall();
subscription = stream.listen(   // `late final StreamSubscription` — assigned here only
```

The stall/total timers are armed (bin/self_manage.dart:203–216) *before* `subscription = stream.listen(...)`, and `subscription` is `late final`. If `listen` ever throws synchronously, the exception propagates out of `_drainBody` before the future is returned; `_drain`'s catch absorbs it and the download proceeds (or fails) normally — but the two already-armed timers stay live. When one fires later, `fail` runs `subscription.cancel()` on the unassigned `late final` and throws `LateInitializationError` as an uncaught async zone error, long after the outcome resolved (stray stack trace on stderr in prod, zone poisoning in tests).

Today's transports (`StreamedResponse` streams from package:http / MockClient) never throw synchronously from `listen`, so this is theoretical hardening, not a bug. Two cheap shapes:

```dart
// option A: nullable field instead of `late final`
StreamSubscription<List<int>>? subscription;
…
void fail(Object error) {
  if (completer.isCompleted) return;
  stallTimer?.cancel();
  totalTimer?.cancel();
  subscription?.cancel();
  completer.completeError(error);
}
```

or arm the timers inside a try that wraps the `listen` call and calls `fail` on the sync path. Optional polish.

💡 **SUGGESTION: the full public contract doc is duplicated verbatim on the private `_BodyDownload` class**

The bullet-list contract ("Headers … stall … total backstop … progress … Range resume … stall is NOT a resume case") appears twice in bin/self_manage.dart: on `final class _BodyDownload` (starting at line 247) and on the public `downloadArchiveBody` (starting at line 444). Same nine bullets, two copies; when the contract changes (e.g. a future "stall IS a resume case after N" policy), both must be edited in lockstep or the private class quietly teaches a different contract than the public API.

Suggestion: keep the full contract on the public function (that is what callers and docs read) and shrink the class doc to its own concern, pointing at the source of truth:

```dart
/// The mutable state of one archive-body download (gh-2043): the bytes
/// received so far, the resume budget, and the full size once known.
/// One attempt = open (headers bound) + drain (stall/backstop bounds).
/// The loop lives in [run]; each decision is a small method so no single
/// function carries the whole complexity (crap4dart ratchet).
/// The behavioral contract (bounds, resume policy, progress) is the one
/// on [downloadArchiveBody].
final class _BodyDownload {
```

Optional polish — no behavioral impact.

